# Shared configuration for Agent 1 (Schema-Intelligence-Layer), Agent 2
# (Data-Profiling-Agent), Agent 3 (Analytics-Agent), and Agent-Orchestrator
# — values genuinely identical across all four. Agent 2 also has its own
# separate .env for DATABASE_URL/etc (deliberately left untouched) but reads
# its Azure OpenAI credentials from here too, same as Agent 1/3.
#
# Each service loads this file as a fallback UNDER its own local .env —
# local always wins on any key present in both. See that service's own
# .env.example for what stays local (ports, per-agent URLs, etc).
#
# ── Azure OpenAI (this copy of the project uses Azure OpenAI, not Groq) ──
# Fill in these 3 values and every service picks them up automatically —
# nothing else to configure. AZURE_OPENAI_ENDPOINT is your resource's base
# URL (e.g. https://your-resource-name.openai.azure.com — no trailing path).
# AZURE_OPENAI_DEPLOYMENT is the *deployment name* you gave the model in
# Azure AI Studio (not necessarily the underlying model's own name).
AZURE_OPENAI_API_KEY=
AZURE_OPENAI_ENDPOINT=
AZURE_OPENAI_DEPLOYMENT=
# Only used by the 3 internal LangGraph tool-routing agents (Agent 1's
# classification router, Agent 2's feature-target and rule-suggestion
# agents) — LangChain's Azure integration needs an explicit api-version,
# unlike the direct narration/classification calls. A recent stable value
# is provided as a default; override only if your resource requires a
# different one.
AZURE_OPENAI_API_VERSION=2024-10-21

# Shared Postgres — see Shared-Postgres/docker-compose.yml (host port 5433,
# mapped from the container's internal 5432 — not 5432 directly).
POSTGRES_HOST=localhost
POSTGRES_PORT=5433
POSTGRES_DB=mva_pipeline
POSTGRES_USER=postgres
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=postgres

LOG_LEVEL=INFO
